description:
User feedback on 2026-10-10: "When I click on the paper, there shouldn't be two tabs as PDF and Details. There shouldn't be tabs either. First of all remove the search index part. This kind of detailed info should go to modal that opens after clicking circled i icon somewhere ... This also includes the 'Metadata sources' thing. Then the current 'Replace the PDF' or 'Upload the PDF' part should be responsible for displaying the PDF. We should also have controls for deleting the PDF so that user can replace it by deleting and reuploading. Overall, we will improve the UX around that part. Use your Impeccable design skills for that."
Design (lead, using the impeccable skill, Operate mode, DESIGN.md 'Category Standard, Played Straight'):
(1) No tabs on desktop. The left panel is always the reader: the PDF when one is attached, otherwise the PDF states (OpenAlex retrieval, DOI fallback, upload, processing steps, failure).
(2) A circled ⓘ icon button labelled 'Paper details' sits beside the title and status badge in the workspace header. It opens a shadcn Sheet: right side on desktop, about 440px wide, overlaying the chat so the PDF stays visible, as Google Drive's ⓘ does; a full-height bottom sheet on mobile. The sheet's sections, in this order:
- title, PaperCitation and the full author list;
- actions: Edit details and Look up again (ARA-095), plus DOI and provider record links;
- the abstract, clamped with Show more;
- identifiers in mono with copy buttons;
- 'Where this information came from' (the former Metadata sources), as a compact field-to-source list;
- an 'Advanced' disclosure holding the former Search index section (status, model, passage count, Rebuild);
- a final destructive 'Delete paper' action (ARA-095).
(3) The reader toolbar gains a PDF menu. Its non-interactive header shows the file name, pages, size and source (Uploaded, or Downloaded from OpenAlex). Its items are Open original, Download, Replace PDF… (the upload dialog; earlier answers keep citing the PDF they used) and Remove PDF… (destructive, behind an AlertDialog). Remove returns the reader to its upload state, so the user can delete and re-upload.
(4) Mobile segments become Chat | PDF only.
(5) Defects found during the design review, fixed here:
- at 390px, chat answer text overflows the viewport horizontally;
- the sticky chat-paper chip row lets scrolled message text show through behind it; it needs an opaque background and a hairline when stuck.
